-- pending: #172 (PinLoop: a self-borrow held across the observations of a looping machine)
-- now: 26:3: this borrow block's body performs the operation 2.0, which resumes: only an abortive operation crosses a borrow's scope (a continuation held across a lend is T1, #172)
-- Coverage: a generator that never ends. `cycle` owns a vector, lends it once, and holds the view
-- across every observation of an endless loop, yielding the vector's elements round and round; the
-- consumer takes seven of them. The lend is made once, before the loop, not again at each step.
--
-- Issue: #172 and #682 (`PinLaw.loop` asserts this shape for a stored machine: `lendLoopApp`, a
-- state that mentions the lend's lifetime; no lender lemma at an observation boundary is proved).
--
-- Rust: `gen move { let v = vec![…]; let s = &v[..]; let mut i = 0; loop { yield s[i % s.len()]; i
-- += 1 } }`, a self-referential coroutine, pinned.
--
-- Today: refused at the borrow block, whose body yields (T1, #172). Both backends stop at the same
-- message.
--
-- Once it lands: i32[1231231]: the elements 1, 2, 3 cycled, seven of them as decimal digits;
-- `live 0` (closing the generator after `take` ends the lend and frees the vector).

include adapters
include slice

fn idx1(i: i32) -> i32 = i + 1

fn cycle() -> 0 with Yield[i32] =
  let buf = array_new[i32](3, idx1);
  borrow imm buf { 'b, v ->
    let mut i = 0;
    loop { yield(at(v, i % 3, 0)); i = i + 1 } }

main -> i32 =
  let mut acc = 0;
  for x in take(cycle(), 7) { acc = acc * 10 + x };
  acc
